## INTRODUCTION
Estradiol (ED) is a major and potent female sex hormone. It is responsible for the development of female reproductive physiology, bone growth, skin health, and exerts an effect on the nervous system. ED also used as a supplement for the production of lean animal meat and enhances milk quantity1-3. It’s been largely excreted from the human body via urine. The fact that the free ED are frequently found in either sewage effluents or in environmental waters is a significant concern to the effect on both aquatic life and public health, such as alterations in psychological or reproductive function and development, and implications in breast tumors and other endocrinal disorders4. Therefore, the detection of ED has importance. Various major methods have been applied to detection ED such as immunoassay5, HPLC6, mass spectrometry7. But the electro-analytical method has many advantages over them such as simple, inherent high selectivity, fast detection, and low cost8-10. In this regard different electrode materials were developed to modify conventional electrodes, such as $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -doped nanoporous carbon11, Pd/Ndoped reduced graphene12, DNA aptamer immobilized gold electrode chip13, enzyme biosensor14, and conductive biochar nanoparticles15. But these materials have their own limitations. Therefore, it important to developed a highly robust, costeffective, electrochemical sensor with better analytical performances.
Metal-organic framework (MOF) is a polymer made of consecutive liked organic and inorganic moieties16, 17. The might be the $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ wonder material after graphene on the unique properties like uniform structure, intrinsic high specific maneuverability. So far, MOFs have been used for several significant applications from catalysis to gas adsorption and separation. Furthermore, the highly uniform morphology of MOF’s able to produce better analytical performance18. However, the inherent poor conductivity of MOFs, as well as the fabrication of stable MOFs structures in aqueous media for electrochemical sensor applications, remains a challenge. In this regard, Zr-porphyrin based MOF has drawn lots of attention, owing to their well defined molecular building blocks and effective square-planar coordination sites within porphyrin units19-22, for excellent catalytic behavior in various reactions such as oxidation, hydrogenation, water-gas shift, electrocatalysis, etc. In a way, the metal co-ordinate in porphyrin struts can act like a single atom and generates an efficient electron transfer channel through hopping mechanism within the MOF structure23-25. Furthermore, the morphology and size of MOFs play a significant role in the electrocatalytic activity. Hollow morphology has shown exclusive applications in the field of the catalyst by acting as nano/microreactor, reproducibility, and reusability with cost-effective. Therefore, nano-size hollow MOF can perform better than their bulk counterparts; due to inherent enrich properties on the basis of their low density, material economy and high surface area with facile mass diffusion26-28. Gold insertions into the porphyrin core can improve the electrical conductivity through hopping mechanism29, 30. Therefore, a post-synthetic gold insertion was done into the porphyrin core present within the MOF. Gold possesses inherent electro-catalytic activity, electrical conductivity, and generate a hospitable environment for biomolecules. Although, the particle size plays a vital for electro-activity and it’s very difficult to get a stable sub-nano size $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ particle. But the porphyrin core within PCN-222 can act as a holder to anchor highly small $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ particles31. Therefore, combined properties of hollow MOF with functionalized sub-nanometer size gold can be better material as an electrochemical sensor for the trace level detection of the biomolecule, ED. Here we have synthesized hollow and solid structured $Z\mathbf{r}$ - porphyrin MOF with PCN-222 structure. The hollow MOF possesses large pore size, high surface area, good chemical stability to water and organic media, and excellent electrocatalytic activities. The anchoring of sub-nanometer size gold particle was done by post-synthetic metal insertion within the porphyrin core in $Z\mathrm{r}$ -porphyrin MOFs by means of a simple hydrothermal process. The sub-nanometer size $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ functionalized hollow PCN-222 modified glassy carbon electrode (AuHPCN-222/GCE) showed better electrochemical characteristics and performances over the same for solid PCN222 (AuSPCN-222/GCE). The amount of Au metal was optimized to get the highest sensitivity. The AuHPCN222/GCE sensor showed excellent electrocatalytic activity towards the ED compared to all other electrodes. The combined properties of hollow structure MOF and catalytic site and charge transfer through hopping mechanism generated by anchored gold particle leads the superior electro-active surface area of the modified electrode and overall performances. The fabricated AuHPCN-222/GCE sensor can provide a potential candidate (bio) electrochemical sensor for ED detection. The established method was applied to detect ED in real samples with excellent recoveries.
## EXPERIMENTAL
Synthesis of hollow PCN-222. HPCN-222 was synthesized according to the previously reported work elsewhere31. Briefly, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of zirconium tetrachloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ with $250~\mathrm{mg}$ of benzoic acid was dissolved in $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethylformamide (DMF) containing and $200\upmu\mathrm{L}\:\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under continuous magnetic stirring for 5 minutes. After that, $10~\mathrm{\mg}$ meso-tetra carboxyphenyl porphyrin (TCPP) was added to the above solution and kept stirring for 10 minutes. The obtained homogeneous solution was transferred to a $12~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflonlined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the HPCN-222 was collected and washed several times ( $7\times10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) with absolute ethanol by centrifugation at $10000\mathrm{rpm}$ .
Synthesis of solid PCN-222. SPCN-222 was synthesized according to the previously reported work32. $35~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ with $1350~\mathrm{mg}$ of benzoic acid and $4~\mathrm{mL}$ DEF were taken into a $20~\mathrm{\mL}$ Pyrex vial. The mixture was dissolved via ultrasonication and kept at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1h. After that, $25~\mathrm{mg}$ of
TCPP was added to the above solution and ultrasonicated till complete dissolution. The obtained solution was kept at 120 $\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}$ oven for $48\mathrm{h}$ . The PCN-222 was isolated via centrifugation and washed with DMF and dipped in acetone for $24\mathrm{h}$ by three repeated cycles. The dark brown crystalline powder was collected after drying in a vacuum at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
Gold functionalization on to HPCN-222/SPCN-222. 20 mg of synthesized HPCN-222/SPCN-222 with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{AuCl}_{4}$ aqueous solution $20~\mathrm{mg.mL^{-1}}$ were added in $4~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and stirred for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . Then the mixture was transferred to a 12 mL Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ .  Finally, the whole mixture was sonicated for $10~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. Then, the products were separated via centrifugation at $10000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ and further purified with ethanol for several times $5{\times}5~\mathrm{mL}$ ).
Activation of synthesized MOFs. All the synthesized materials were activated by the same process to remove the un-reacted species and DMF. Typically, each of them was $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ dispersed in methanol for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and after that acetone for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the removal of acetone by centrifugation, the sample was activated by drying under vacuum at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24h. The active samples were then used for all the electrochemical studies and characterizations.
Synthesis of gold nanoparticles. $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $2\%\mathrm{(w/v)}$ trisodium citrate was added rapidly to a refluxing $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of $1\%$ (w/v) $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ solution . The reaction was continued until the color turned dark red. After that, the mixture was cooled to room temperature and was stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for further use. Spherical shaped Au nanoparticles with a diameter of less than $20~\mathrm{nm}$ were achieved (Figure S1).
Preparation of electrodes. The glassy carbon electrode (3 mm diameter) was cleaned by polished with $0.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and 0.05 $\upmu\mathrm{m}\mathrm{m}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ slurries on chamois leather. After that, the GCE was sonicated in distilled water and ethanol for $2~\mathrm{min}$ to obtain a mirror-like surface. Then ${\mathrm{~5~}}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of a water dispersion of AuHPCN-222 $(1\ \mathrm{mg.mL^{-1}}),$ ) was dropped on the GCE surface and dried in a desiccator. All the other electrodes were made in a similar way.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterizations.
Scheme 1 presents the graphical representation of postmetalation by Au within the MOF structure. The MOFs were characterized by powder $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD). As displayed in Figure 1a, the main characteristic diffraction peaks are at 4.9, 7.2, 8.37 and 9.79 degrees. The obtained PXRD patterns were matched perfectly with previously reported methods corresponding to the PCN-222 MOF structure. This is indicating a successful synthesis of the respective MOFs. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns are in good agreement with those reported in the previous literature, indicating the successful synthesis of the PCN-22231, 33. The inset PXRD within Figure 1a of AuHPCN222 and AuSPCN-222 is clearly indicating the incorporation of Au with complete retention of individual MOF structure. The crystal size of the incorporated Au has been calculated by Scherrer’s equation (1)
In equation (1) $\lambda$ is $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K a}$ radiation wavelength $0.1544~\mathrm{nm}$ and $\beta$ is full width at half maximum in radians. The calculated average particle sizes from the respective Au PXRD peaks were found to be 0.245 and $0.187{\mathrm{nm}}$ .
The chemical composition of the AuHPCN-222 was investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The XPS spectrum from 0 to $1300.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ depicted a characteristic binding energy signature of Zr 3d, C 1s, N 1s, O 1s, Au 4f
S1). The high-resolution spectrum of the Au 4f spectrum (Figure 1c) can be attributed to the binding energies of 83.59, $87.23~\mathrm{~eV}$ which corresponded to porphyrin co-ordinated $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ . These results indicate the successful functionalization of $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ into the MOF structure31. The Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms (Figure 1d) measured at $77~\mathrm{~K~}$ showed a typical type IV isotherm indicates the presence of mesopores. The BET surface area was calculated as $1520\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $957\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for HPCN-222 and AuHPCN-222 respectively. The decrease in surface area can be counted on the basis of mass occupied by Au after functionalization. The corresponding pore size distribution was calculated by Saito–Foley (SF) method. Pore-size distribution (inset Figure 1d) was calculated by the, indicating the existence of both micropores $\left[0.64\ \mathrm{nm}\right]$ and mesopores $(2.4~\mathrm{nm})$ ) simultaneously. The ED molecule can easily diffuse into the pore of AuHPCN-222 and access maximum active sites. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) AuHPCN222 and AuSPCN-222 were depicted in Figure 2a-c. Figure 1a, c with inset HAADF-STEM images showed uniform hollow porous tube morphology of AuHPCN-222. A careful observation of high-resolution HRTEM images demonstrates that the hollow tube structure is made by the stacking of several smaller nanocubes (Figure S2). Elemental mapping spectra (Figure 2d) using energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) illustrate the homogeneous distribution of Zr, C, N, O, and Au throughout the entire hollow structure. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ particles were not visible even on HRTEM image besides clear evidence from elemental mapping, PXRD and XPS analysis. It may be an indication for the formation of sub-nanometer size $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ particles through anchoring within the co-ordinated sites of porphyrin moiety of HPCN-22222, 33. This is in good agreement with sizes calculated by Scherrer’s formula.
Electrochemical studies.
A comparative study on the electrochemical properties of the electrodes was done by cyclic voltammetry in a standard $5\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{-3}/[F e(C N)_{6}]^{-4}}$ solution. The $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{4-}[F e(C N)_{6}]^{3-}}$ redox system eventually works as a prob. The order of obtained anodic current values is as follows: AuHPCN222/GCE $\mathrm{(126~\muA)^{>}}$ AuSPCN-222/GCE $(112\phantom{-}\upmu\mathrm{A})>$ AuNP/GCE $(109\ \upmu\mathrm{A}){>}\mathrm{HPCN-}222/\mathrm{GCE}$ $(107~\upmu\mathrm{A})^{\rightarrow}$ bare GCE $(65~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ ). The increase in oxidation current mainly depends on the electrocatalytic activity and charge transferability of the electrode. The functionalized sub-nanometre size Au within the MOF hugely enhances the electro-catalytic sites and charge transfer through hopping mechanism23. Consequently, the AuHPCN-222/GCE sensor was shown more than a 2-time increase in anodic current value than bare GCE.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is a perfect tool for the investigation of interfacial changes of electrochemical properties at the surface of the electrode. Fig. 3b shows the Nyquist plot of all the individual modified GCE electrodes and bare GCE in the frequency of $0.1{-}10\mathrm{kHz}$ . As a representative of the electron transfer resistance $\left(\operatorname{R}_{\operatorname{ct}}\right)$ , the semicircle part is impacted by the dielectric and insulating features at the electrode-electrolyte interface34. It can be seen that the $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ value of $141\ \Omega$ for bare GCE decreased to $85~\Omega$ after the immobilization of HPCN-222 on the GCE. The same for AuSPCN-222, AuHPCN-222, and AuNP/GCE are 63, 50 and $66~\Omega$ , respectively. The decrease in $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ value may be ascribed to the enhancement of electron mobility through the hopping mechanism within the $\mathrm{MOF^{29}}$ . And the subnanometer size Au particle formation within the MOFs structures was useful to generate a bit higher charge transfer property than large-sized AuNP. Therefore, the charge transfer rate follows the order as AuHPCN-222/GCE $>$ AuSPCN222/GCE>AuNP/GCE> HPCN-222/GCE>bare GCE. All these above discussed prosperities indicate that the AuHPCN222 can be used for the detection of ED. Certainly, the high surface area with a hollow structure and sub-nanometer size Au nanoparticle grafted hollow MOF structure was abled to surplus the AuNP performances.
