The electro-catalytic activity of each electrode towards ED was analyzed using cyclic voltammetry (CV) technique. The redox behavior of the ED at all the electrodes was alike.  ED showed an irreversible oxidation CV curve at all the three electrodes. The AuHPCN-222/GCE showed the highest electrocatalytic activity among all three electrodes with a relative negative shift of $61~\mathrm{mV}$ , $50~\mathrm{mV}$ , $48~\mathrm{mV}$ , and $31~\mathrm{mV}$ compare to AuNP/GCE, bare GCE, HPCN-222/GCE, and AuSPCN-222, respectively. Furthermore, AuHPCN-222/GCE produced an enhanced anodic peak current among all the electrodes. The relative increase in anodic current value at AuHPCN-222/GCE was 3.2, 1.5, 1.45, and 1.2 times higher than bare GCE, HPCN-222/GCE, AuNP/GCE, and AuSPCN222, respectively. The anodic oxidation peak at AuHPCN222/GCE is sharper than AuNP/GCE. This indicates better diffusion of at the AuHPCN-222/GCE electrode. Contrary, the consequence of lower diffusion leads to poor sensitivity at AuNP/GCE. The better electrocatalytic performances of AuHPCN-222/GCE can be attributed to the presence of porphyrin linked $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ atom within the HPCN-222. Here Au has played a vital role by providing the high-density catalytic adsorption sites for ED on the sensor surface and electron transfer pathway through hopping mechanism20, 30. And the hollow structure MOF favored diffusion during the electrochemical process. The mechanism of electron transfer from the surface adsorbs ED to the electrode can occur through the generation of a hole at a porphyrin linker during the application of positive potential, which eventually transferred to the Au catalytic centers. Also, this is supported by the relative negative shift of anodic oxidation potentials. The electro-oxidation current mainly depends on the adsorbed amount of analyte and interfacial charge transfer.  Therefore the combined effect of charge transfer through Au channel within the MOF with better surface adsorption $17.4~\mathrm{nM.cm^{-2}}$ ) leads to the enhancement of anodic peak current36.
The experimental pH plays a significant on the charge transfer and redox reaction during electrocatalytic oxidation. Therefore, the optimum pH was found by varying the pH from 3 to 9 and recording the complimentary anodic current. ED has shown a negative shift of anodic oxidation potential $\mathrm{(E_{p})}$ with increasing pH. This indicates that the involvement of proton(s) in the anodic oxidation during anodic oxidation (Figure 3d). The Ep vs. pH plot follow a straight linear line with corresponding linear equation $\mathrm{E_{p}}$ , ED $\mathrm{(V)=-0.054\pH+}$ 0.8799 $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.997.$ ). The slope of the straight line is in good agreement with the theoretical value of $0.059\mathrm{~V~}$ predicted by
Nernst’s equation37. This is the satisfying condition for the transfer of an equal number of electron and proton. Therefore, the electro-oxidation of ED undergoes via two electrons and two proton transfer process. ED showed the highest electrocatalytic current at $\mathsf{p H}7$ . Further investigations were carried out at that optimum pH (Figure 3d).
