Determination of ED. Differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) method has an inherent capability to minimize the capacitive background current, which eventually enhances the sensitivity. Therefore quantitative measurements of ED in the optimized condition were done by DPV. From Figure 5a, it is clear that the anodic peak currents increase with increasing ED concentration. DPV peaks currents follow a linear relation with concentrations from 0.01 to $220~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with two different linear regions (Figure 5b). The corresponding linear equations (6 and 7) are as follow
The sensitivity for the $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ linear rang was $2.2~\upmu\mathrm{A}.\upmu\mathrm{M}.\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and that for $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ linear range $0.3\ \upmu\mathrm{A.\upmuM.\mathrm{cm}^{-2}}$ . The two sets of sensitivity values are due to the formation of monolayer followed by multilayer adsorption on the sensor surface40.
The measured limit of detection (m-LOD) was $0.5{\mathrm{~nM}}$ . The obtained analytical performances were compared with previously reported works (Table 2). The dominance in a wider linear determination range with lower m-LOD indicates the superiority of the fabricated sensor among them. The basis of these excellent analytical results is due to (i) the high specific surface area AuHPCN-222 $(951~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ), can produce abundant electro-active sites and mass transport for ED, improve the sensor response; (ii) the aromatic ring and hydroxyl groups of ED can interact with porphyrin through $\scriptstyle{\pi-}$ π, H-bonding bimetallic metalloporphyrin MOF. This intern facilities the surface adsorption; (iii) the bio-accessibility of Au helps to enhance absorption41 (iv) the Au channel within the MOF enhanced the charge transfer from the ED to the electrode surface.
Repeatability and reproducibility. Repeatability of AuHPCN-222/GCE was found by taking 10 successive DPV measurements in $0.05~\mathrm{mM}$ ED solution at $\mathsf{p H}7$ (Figure S3a). The calculated relative standard deviation (R.S.D) for the measurements was $0.78\%$ . This indicates excellent repeatability with precision. While 5 different AuHPCN222/GCE electrodes were prepared and applied for DPV analysis in a $0.05\mathrm{mM}$ ED solution $\mathrm{\pH\7}_{,}$ ). The R.S.D for the reproducibility analysis was calculated as $0.85\%$ (Figure S3b). All these above discussions supported the excellent repeatability and reproducibility of the fabricated sensor. Furthermore, the sensor was kept at ambient temperature for 20 days while taking DPV of $0.05~\mathrm{mM}$ ED $\left(\mathrm{pH}\ 7\right.$ ) each day. There was no significant change in the anodic peak current values (Figure S4). Therefore, the sensor has good stability and robustness.
Interference and real sample analysis. The selectivity of the prepared sensor was investigated against the common organic and inorganic interfering species such as $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}$ , Cl-, ascorbic acid, urea, dopamine, uric acid, tyramine, tryptamine, glucose, bisphenol A, atrazine, parathion. The tolerance level was decided on the basis of recovery $0.05\mathrm{\mM}$ ED in the presence maximum concentration of interferer causing interference up to $\pm5.0\%$ . The sensor was not shown any large deviations within the taken concentration (1000-fold for inorganic species, and 300-fold organic species) of the interferers (Figure S5). The above discussion justifies the proper selectivity of the prepared AuHPCN-222/GCE sensor and encourages it to be applied in the biological and pharmaceutical analysis. For practical sample analysis, the fabricated sensor was employed to detect ED in human urine and tablet formulation. Validation of the analysis was found by the standard addition method. The human urine sample was collected from Yangzhou University hospital and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\mathrm{~l~ml}$ urine sample was diluted 20-times with PBS $\mathrm{(pH~7)}$ ) for stock. For pharmaceuticals formulation, $30~\mathrm{ED}$ tablet (2 mg) was ground and dissolved in $250~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol to make a stock. Appropriate dilution was done with PBS $\left(\mathrm{pH}\ 7\right)$ ) according to the requirement. For the analysis, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ from the stock solution was added to $9\ \mathrm{\mL}$ of PBS $\mathrm{(pH~}7)$ . The recovery of the spiked ED in the investigating sample was calculated and presented in Table S2, 3. It was observed that the recoveries were in the range of $98.0\%-103.0\%$ and the R.S.D of each analysis was less than $3.5\%$ . Therefore, the AuHPCN-222/GCE sensor was highly effective in practical sample analysis.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ anchored hollow $Z\mathrm{r}$ -porphyrin MOF with PCN-222 structure was synthesized and excellent electrochemical properties of the material were investigated. Enhancement in the charge transfer with bio-accessible surface supported adsorption of ED on the AuHPCN-222/GCE sensor was explored. Furthermore, the hollow structured MOF showed better electrocatalytic performances than solid structured MOF, especially in terms of electro-catalytic and diffusion. The introduction of sub-nanometer size Au of hollow PCN-222  by the post metalation method effectively improved the charge transport to the electroactive sites provided than Au nanoparticles. Electro-kinetics parameters of ED oxidation at the sensor surface was enhanced with high charge transfer and electron transfer rate constant. By combining the merits of functionalized $\mathrm{Au}(0)$ with a hollow structure, we were able to obtain an excellent electrochemical performance towards ED sensing. Moreover, the fabricated (bio)-electrochemical sensor exhibited advantages on the wider linear range nM to $\upmu\mathrm{M}$ range $(0.01{-}210~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and trace level LOD $(0.5\mathrm{\nM})$ ), excellent selectivity with reasonable reproducibility and stability. The sensor was displayed excellent anti-interference with selectivity as well as reasonable recovery in urine samples. All the above discussion indicates that the AuHPCN-222/GCE sensor has the great potential for clinical analysis of ED in diagnostic samples.
